<section class="block">
<h2>Who this is for</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Product startups</strong> that need senior architecture and engineering leadership as they move from product-market fit to scale.</li>
<li><strong>Global capability centres</strong> building AI, data and platform teams for parent companies in the US, Europe or the Gulf.</li>
<li><strong>High-volume consumer and fintech platforms</strong> where reliability and cost per transaction decide margins.</li>
<li><strong>Investors</strong> backing Indian technology companies.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>What shapes technology decisions in India</h2>
<p>The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 is now being brought into force: the DPDP Rules were notified on 13 November 2025, with obligations phasing in over 18 months<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Consent-manager registration follows after a year, and most core duties for data fiduciaries apply from mid-2027<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. The engineering implication is consent and purpose tracking at the data level, retention that actually deletes, and breach detection you can evidence.</p>
<table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Rule or standard</th><th>Who it touches</th><th>What it means for architecture</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>DPDP Act and Rules</td><td>Data fiduciaries processing digital personal data</td><td>Consent and notice records per purpose, retention and erasure jobs, breach detection and reporting</td></tr><tr><td>Consent managers</td><td>Platforms integrating registered consent managers</td><td>Interoperable consent APIs and audit trails</td></tr><tr><td>Customer and parent-company requirements</td><td>GCCs and exporters of software services</td><td>SOC 2, ISO 27001 and client security reviews built into delivery</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Related reading: <a href="/insights/idempotent-payments-order-pipelines/">idempotent payment pipelines</a>, <a href="/insights/team-topologies-for-ai-teams/">team topologies for AI teams</a> and <a href="/insights/llm-model-routing/">LLM model routing for cost</a>.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Typical first projects</h2>
<ol>
<li>A DPDP-ready data architecture: consent, purpose, retention and breach handling.</li>
<li>Scaling a platform to very high volume with predictable latency and cost.</li>
<li>Standing up an AI platform team in a global capability centre.</li>
<li>Production RAG and agents with evaluation and cost control.</li>
</ol>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Time zones</h2>
<p>I am based in India as well as the United States and the UAE, so Indian teams get my full working day. I also bridge Indian teams with US, European and Gulf stakeholders through clear written decisions and overlapping review slots.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>How engagements work</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Architecture and AI readiness review</strong> (2 to 4 weeks): a clear picture of the current platform, risks and a prioritised roadmap.</li>
<li><strong>Fractional CTO</strong> (ongoing, part-time): technical strategy, architecture governance, hiring and board-level communication.</li>
<li><strong>Build and hand-off</strong>: hands-on delivery of a platform or AI capability, documented so your team can run it.</li>
<li><strong>Technical due diligence</strong>: an independent view of a target's technology for investors and acquirers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every engagement starts with a free 30-minute call. See <a href="/how-i-work/">how I work</a> for the full process.</p>
</section>
